The European Commission is in talks with Apple over rules that developers say stop them from offering browsers powered by their own technology on the iPhone.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/03/european-commission.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-995806" /><br/>
The discussions follow an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/23/coalition-of-eu-businesses-challenges-apple/">open letter published yesterday</a> by Mozilla, Shopify, and a coalition representing up to 55,000 EU businesses and organizations. They argue that Apple has technically opened the door to competing browser technology, but made it too difficult to use in practice.<br/>
<br/>
The dispute concerns what happens beneath a browser's interface. Firefox on the iPhone looks different from Safari, but both use Apple's WebKit engine to display websites. Mozilla wants to use its own engine, Gecko, which powers Firefox on the Mac and would let it bring additional Firefox features to the iPhone.<br/>
<br/>
Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2024/01/25/third-party-default-browsers-eu-ios-17-4/">began allowing alternative engines</a> in the EU with iOS 17.4. Mozilla <a href="https://blog.mozilla.org/en/mozilla/internet-policy/apple-ios-browser-engine-rules/">argues Apple's conditions</a> mean it cannot simply switch existing Firefox users to Gecko via an app update. It would need to release a second Firefox app, then persuade users to download it and manually move their bookmarks, passwords, and browsing history.<br/>
<br/>
The coalition says no browser maker has managed to move its existing EU users from WebKit to its own engine more than two and a half years after Apple's deadline to comply with the Digital Markets Act (DMA). It wants the European Commission to open formal proceedings and force Apple to remove the alleged barriers.<br/>
<br/>
Responding to the letter, a European Commission spokesperson told <em>POLITICO</em>'s Fair Play newsletter that it is "currently in dialogue with Apple." The Commission is also consulting the signatories about how rival browsers can compete with Safari on Apple devices. It described browser competition as a DMA priority because browsers determine how people access the web and how publishers reach their readers.<br/>
<br/>
Apple says its <a href="https://developer.apple.com/support/alternative-browser-engines/">requirements for competing engines</a> protect users from malicious websites and other security threats. Developers seeking approval must meet privacy and security standards and commit to fixing vulnerabilities promptly.<div class="linkback">Tags: <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/european-commission/">European Commission</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/european-union/">European Union</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/politico/">Politico</a></div><br/>This article, &quot;<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/24/eu-talks-with-apple-over-rival-iphone-browsers/">EU in Talks With Apple Over Barriers to Rival iPhone Browsers</a>&quot; first appeared on <a href="https://www.macrumors.com">MacRumors.com</a><br/><br/><a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/threads/eu-in-talks-with-apple-over-barriers-to-rival-iphone-browsers.2495117/">Discuss this article</a> in our forums<br/><br/>